2026-10-30 — Phase 3a: recommendation-only action planner

- Added `src/pagent/orchestrator.py` to request one structured recommendation from the current
  scope-derived action registry, with `stop` and `ask_human` control choices.
- Validated the model response against the exact current registry and resolved the action back to
  its trusted `ActionSpec`; model output cannot provide tool arguments.
- Added interactive agent menu option `[10]` to display a recommendation without executing it.
- Kept target response evidence in the existing untrusted-context channel. Recommendations still
  require a person to run the action through the existing policy and approval paths.
- Updated README documentation. No tests were added or run in this increment.

2026-10-30 — Phase 3b: confirmed single-action handoff

- Connected read-only batch recommendations to their existing `Session` handlers.
- Added a per-recommendation `y/N` confirmation. Declining leaves the session unchanged.
- Kept targeted/approval-gated recommendations out of the dispatcher; the planner points to the
  existing intrusive menu and its approval flow instead.
- Reject planner rationales containing terminal control characters before display.
- Updated README and interactive help. No tests were added or run in this increment.

2026-10-30 — Orchestrator architecture plan

- Added `ORCHESTRATOR_PLAN.md` to define a scope-bound path toward higher routine-work automation.
- Specified target selection as a match against an existing authorization scope, never as authority
  by itself; scope cannot expand from discovery.
- Defined the proposed chronological action ledger, bounded routine supervisor, queued exact-action
  approvals, safe resume behavior, stop conditions, and evaluation metrics.
- Recorded the implementation order: persist run state and ledger before multi-step continuation.
- No runtime behavior changed and no tests were added or run.

2026-10-30 — Phase 3c: persistent action ledger

- Added an append-only `action_events` table to the existing SQLite store for planner decisions,
  operator confirmations, dispatch lifecycle, and handoffs.
- Built a chronological ledger view by combining those events with stored tool-call metadata and
  finding evidence references; response bodies are excluded.
- Added the ledger to JSON/Markdown reports, including a Markdown table with timestamps, tools,
  targets, decisions, outcomes, and linked finding IDs.
- Recorded per-action confirmation and dispatch lifecycle from the interactive planner.
- Updated README and orchestrator milestones. No tests were added or run in this increment.

2026-10-30 — Ledger status classification fix

- Separated successful tool calls, execution failures, pending approvals, and policy denials in
  the report ledger. A failed network connection is no longer labeled as a policy denial.
- Existing generated reports are left untouched; the corrected label applies to reports generated
  after this change. No tests were run.

2026-10-30 — Loopback lab routine

- Added `--lab-routine` to run availability preflight, fixed content discovery, configured crawl
  seeds, and a capped one-hop scan of same-host JavaScript references in one launch.
- Restricted the mode to one currently valid loopback host and one port, with only `http_head`,
  `http_get`, and `dir_probe`; it rejects CIDRs, intrusive targets, broader tools, and unbounded
  seed/prefix lists.
- JavaScript fetching is capped at 10 same-host `.js` paths, uses a transient derived scope without
  changing the scope file, and never follows off-host links or extracted endpoints. Existing JS
  signal handling redacts credential-shaped values.
- The routine stops if a stage's tool calls all fail and records stage lifecycle in the action log.
- Updated README and the architecture plan. No tests were added or run.

2026-10-30 — Evidence-driven local brain loop

- Changed the one-command loopback mode from a fixed stage sequence to a model-driven selection
  loop. After each action, the model sees updated findings and bounded evidence, then chooses the
  next remaining registered read-only batch action or stops/asks for a human.
- Requires an Ollama model and performs no per-action confirmation; launching `--lab-brain` is the
  explicit operator start. `--lab-routine` remains an alias.
- Keeps the loop restricted to one valid loopback host/port and read-only registered actions;
  intrusive actions are excluded. Automatic finding review/proposals remain proposal-only.
- Updated README and architecture plan. No tests were added or run.

2026-10-30 — AI review visibility and limits

- The action ledger now records AI finding-triage decisions, including the reasoning model,
  reviewed finding IDs, per-finding notes, IDs prioritized for further review, and review failures.
- Preserved the reviewer's bounded per-finding notes in the proposal report rather than dropping
  them after triage in both the interactive agent and standalone proposal command; added
  duplicate/unknown finding reference rejection.
- Documented that AI triage prioritizes findings and does not confirm their truth; narrow
  deterministic repeat checks remain the verification mechanism.
- Updated the brain-mode and architecture documentation to describe the AI role accurately.
  No tests were added or run.
